# Conventional Commits

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) for every git commit subject, PR title, and PR merge commit message.

## Format

```text
<type>[optional scope]: <description>
```

Allowed types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`

## Rules

- Description: imperative mood, lowercase start, no trailing period
- Keep the subject ~72 characters or less
- Scope in parentheses when useful (e.g. `feat(auth): ...`)
- Breaking changes: `!` after type/scope and/or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer
- Optional body after a blank line for why/context
- PR titles use the same format as the commit subject
- PR merge messages (squash/merge commit subject) use the same Conventional Commits format as the PR title; do not use GitHub defaults like `Merge pull request #123 from ...`

## Branch protection

- Never push commits directly to `main` (or `master`)
- Land changes only via pull request merge
- Do not `git push origin main` / `git push origin HEAD:main` unless the user explicitly overrides this rule

## Examples

```text
# ✅ GOOD
feat(lessons): add spaced repetition queue
fix: correct TTS timeout handling
feat(api)!: remove legacy lesson schema

# ❌ BAD
Added new feature
Update stuff.
FEAT: New API
Merge pull request #42 from alice/feat-lessons
```
